---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import WaysIn from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/WaysIn.astro";
import Numbers from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/Numbers.astro";
import PlaceSpark from "@daviddef/archive-kit/components/PlaceSpark.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import atlas from "../../public/atlas-data.json";
import ppl from "../data/people.json";
import reg from "../data/register.json";
import searched from "../data/searched.json";
import corrections from "../data/corrections.json";
import places from "../data/places.json";
/* Six tiles, all of them measures of the work. Five of the seven that used to
   sit here were years out of the story — 1820, 1779, 1915, 1905, 1840 — and
   every one is a better line of prose than it was a number: they are quotations
   and turning points, and they are all still on the page, in the cards and the
   documents section where a reader can see what they say. A tile that reads
   1840 says nothing about how far this archive has got.

   Counted at build time so they stay true as the work grows. */
const searches = searched.sets.reduce((n, g) => n + (g.rows || []).length, 0);
const stats = [
  [ppl.people.length, "people published, each on a page of their own", "/people/"],
  [reg.rows.length.toLocaleString(), "register entries read and set out one by one", "/register/"],
  [searches, "searches written down, most of which found nothing — a search is only worth anything written down", "/searched/"],
  [corrections.rows.length, "claims this archive has corrected, with what caused each", "/corrections/"],
  [places.places.length, "places named, with the people this archive can put in each", "/places/"],
  ["646", "people in the whole world carry the spelling Booyzen — against 53,764 Booysens", "/name/"],
];
---
<Base title="The Booyzen Archive"
      eyebrow="Graaff-Reinet · Cradock · Barkly East · Johannesburg · Brisbane"
      dek="A Cape Dutch family that kept marrying British women, changed one letter of its name in a war, and came off the train at Johannesburg with nothing."
      bare={true}>

<section class="hero">
  <img src={u("/hero-stormberg.jpg")}
       srcset={`${u("/hero-stormberg-sm.jpg")} 1200w, ${u("/hero-stormberg.jpg")} 2400w`}
       sizes="100vw" loading="eager" decoding="async"
       alt="Winter veld of the north-eastern Cape at first light, a farm track running towards a flat-topped escarpment, a single small stone-walled kraal in the middle distance." />
  <div class="cap">
    <div class="kick">A family history, kept honestly</div>
    <h1>One letter of this surname was changed on purpose, and the reason was a war.</h1>
    <p class="sub">Graaff-Reinet · Cradock · Barkly East · Johannesburg — a Cape Dutch family that kept
      marrying British women, and came off the train at the Rand with nothing.</p>
  </div>
</section>
<p class="cap" style="margin-top:10px">A modern illustration, not a photograph, and not a picture of any
  farm this family is known to have held — it stands for the country below the <b>Stormberg</b> between
  Barkly East and Dordrecht, where they were living when the war came. There is no photograph of their
  farm, and this archive does not invent one.</p>

<section class="opener">
  <p class="lede">
    This archive was built for <b>David Defranceski</b>, born in Johannesburg, living in Brisbane. It runs
    backwards from his grandmother <b>Catherine Mary Sophia Booyzen</b> (1929–2013) — who married
    <b>Nuno Fernando Lerena</b> at St Joseph's, Mayfair, in 1949 — into the north-eastern Cape: to
    <b>Barkly East</b> and <b>Dordrecht</b>, to <b>Cradock</b> and <b>Graaff-Reinet</b>, and out again to
    <b>Württemberg</b>, to <b>Soho</b>, and to a <b>County Cork</b> that may or may not be real.
  </p>
  <p class="lede" style="margin-top:14px">
    It is a public archive with one rule: <b>the living are left out</b> — omitted, not hidden. And a
    second rule, which is harder: every claim says how well it is known.
    <span class="chip doc" style="padding:2px 8px;font-size:10px">Documented</span>
    <span class="chip inf" style="padding:2px 8px;font-size:10px">Inferred</span>
    <span class="chip lore" style="padding:2px 8px;font-size:10px">Family lore</span>
  </p>

  <WaysIn doors={[
    { href: u("/name/"), name: "Why Booyzen, not Booysen", blurb: "The family says the spelling was changed in the Anglo-Boer War to avoid being taken for men who fought on the British side. The surname statistics agree with them.", cta: "The name →" },
    { href: u("/rebels/"), name: "Read the testimony", blurb: "A farm burned, a man shot, women and children in a camp, and a train to Johannesburg with nothing but a pumpkin. Seven claims, tested one at a time.", cta: "The rebels →" },
    { href: u("/documents/"), name: "Read the documents", blurb: "Civil records, church registers and Master's Office files the family tree had carried for years without anyone transcribing them. They corrected several dates, killed one legend, and caught the surname changing.", cta: "The documents →" },
  ]} />
</section>

<section class="blk">
  
  <section class="feat">
    <h2 style="margin-top:6px">Where this family can actually be put</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:10px">From the Karoo to the Rand, and out to a County Cork that may or may not be real. <strong>Every dot is a place this archive can put a named person in, and its size is how many stand there.</strong> The biggest are labelled; the colours are this archive's own regions. Nothing is a map — there is no coastline here, because this archive has not got one.</p>
  <PlaceSpark places={atlas.places} href={u("/atlas/")}
              label={`${atlas.places.length} places, from the Karoo to the Rand — the size of a dot is how many people stand in it`} />
  </section>
<h2>What the family remembers, and how it has held up</h2>
  <p class="dek">One long piece of testimony sits at the centre of this archive — recorded on the family
    tree from <b>Tersia Booyzen</b>, told to her by her father. It carries seven separate claims. None of
    them has yet been proved. Two are already in trouble, and three now have strong circumstantial
    support that did not exist before this archive was built.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:22px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:17rem">The claim</th><th>Where it stands</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>The family were Cape Rebels</strong></td>
          <td><span class="chip doc">Yes — but under the other surname</span> Of the
            <strong>26 Booysens</strong> on the roll not one is from this district, and
            <strong>Booyzen returns nothing at all</strong>. The surname <strong>BARRY</strong> had never
            been searched. It returns four, and two are here: <strong>Fred J. Barry of Ivanhoe, Barkly
            East / Wodehouse, tried June 1901</strong> — the same month as <strong>Daniel Willem Schoeman
            of Caerlaverock</strong>, in whose house the family married five weeks later — and
            <strong>Richard Peter Barry of Jerpoint</strong>, which is Kathleen Barry's brother's name.
            Identity unproven; the district is not.
            <a href={u("/rebels/")}>The rebels &rarr;</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>The spelling was changed to avoid the "joiners"</strong></td>
          <td><span class="chip doc">The change is on paper. The spelling was not new.</span> On the
            <strong>1905 death form the clerk writes Boo<em>y</em>sen and the dead man's son signs
            Boo<em>y</em>zen</strong>, four lines apart — though what carries weight there is the
            <strong>signature</strong>, not the contrast, since Cape clerks of 1904&ndash;07 demonstrably
            spelled one Middelburg constable both ways in the same registry. And the z was
            <strong>never invented</strong>:
            the Cape archives hold <strong>39 documents under BOOYZEN, the earliest 1779</strong> — a
            hundred and twenty years before the war — and an 1851 register writes the family
            <em>Booyzen</em> in the same hand that writes the godparents <em>Pieterzen</em>. As for the
            motive, the joiners register holds <strong>one</strong> Booysen in the country, and he is a
            Free State man from Heilbron. <a href={u("/name/")}>The name &rarr;</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>A forebear was shot and killed by the British</strong></td>
          <td><span class="chip lore">Disproved for both candidates</span> Petrus Jacobus died in
            <strong>1884</strong>, fifteen years before the war. And <strong>Willem Hermanus died of an
            ulcer of the stomach</strong> at Indwe on 13 September 1905, after fourteen months' illness,
            under a doctor's care — the death registration has now been read. Whoever the story means,
            <strong>it is neither of them</strong>. <a href={u("/documents/")}>The document &rarr;</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>The farm was burned out</strong></td>
          <td><span class="chip inf">Support, and a complication</span> He is a <strong>Boer</strong> —
            farmer, of Lady Grey — at his marriage in 1879, and a <strong>Mason</strong> at his death in
            1905; a man who works land and then works stone has often lost the land, and the war sits in
            that interval. But his father's 1884 estate leaves <strong>“moveables only, such as wearing
            apparel”</strong>, so this household was landless <strong>fifteen years before the war</strong>.
            Any account beginning with a prosperous farm being burned has to account for 1884 first.
            <a href={u("/documents/")}>The documents &rarr;</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>The women and children were put in a concentration camp</strong></td>
          <td><span class="chip lore">Searched, and not found</span> The University of Cape Town's camp
            database has now been searched: <strong>Barry returns nothing at all</strong>, and the five
            Booyzens in it are <strong>every one Transvaal</strong>. But that database is built largely
            from camp <em>death</em> lists — it records who died far better than who was interned and
            walked out — so the claim is <strong>unsupported, not disproved</strong>.
            <a href={u("/searched/")}>What was searched &rarr;</a></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>They came to Johannesburg and the boys went onto the mines</strong></td>
          <td><span class="chip inf">Half-confirmed, and the mines half is not holding</span> The move is
            certain: the birthplaces run <strong>Barkly East, Indwe, then Johannesburg</strong>, and the
            family's estates are all in the <strong>Transvaal</strong>. But of the occupations this
            archive has actually read for these men — <strong>boer then mason, tree nurseryman, clerk on
            the South African Railways</strong> — <strong>not one is a miner</strong>, and no Booyzen
            mining record appears anywhere in the national archives index.
            <a href={u("/searched/")}>What was searched &rarr;</a></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Why this archive exists</h2>
  <p class="dek">In 1915 a man at Barkly East filled in his own mother's death notice. Where it asked for
    her parents' names, he wrote:</p>
  <blockquote style="font-size:1.06em;margin-top:18px">
    "Father: <strong>Kolbe, thinks James was christian name</strong>.<br />
    Mother: <strong>Unknown</strong>."
  </blockquote>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:20px">
    <p>His grandfather was <b>George Augustus Kolbe</b> — who married at sixteen on a sworn lie in
      Shoreditch, sailed on the <em>Nautilus</em> in 1819 as an <b>1820 Settler</b>, preached across the
      Orange River, and named a Karoo farm after the Württemberg his own father had left. His grandmother
      was <b>Margaret Downing</b> of London.</p>
    <p>Both of them lie under a cut-stone monument at <b>Burgersdorp</b> — names, London birthplaces,
      farm and dates carved into it — about forty miles from where that notice was written.</p>
    <blockquote>The family forgot them inside seventy years, with the headstone still standing.</blockquote>
    <p><b>And it had happened before, on the other side of the house.</b> In 1840 a relative of
      <b>James Montjoy</b> filled in the same printed form at the same box. He knew the dead man's trade,
      his house, what it was worth, the village he died in and the name of his wife's first husband. Where
      it asked for the parents, he wrote:</p>
    <blockquote style="font-size:1.06em">"Names of the Parents of the Deceased. <strong>Not
      Known</strong>." <span class="cap" style="display:block;margin-top:6px">— and for birth-place, only
      <strong>"Europe"</strong>.</span></blockquote>
    <p>James Montjoy had died <b>thirteen years earlier</b>. That is the oldest document in this archive
      and the top of that line, and it is where the line stops — not because the records run out, but
      because the family's own memory had already run out by 1840.
      <a href={u("/documents/")}>The 1840 notice &rarr;</a></p>
    <p>Everything on this site was recovered from documents that were <b>already in the family's own
      tree</b>, scanned and attached and never once transcribed.
      <a href={u("/documents/")}>The documents &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>


<section class="blk">
  <h2>The shape of it</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Strip the archive to one sentence and it is this: <b>a Cape Dutch male line that married British
      women for three generations running</b>, and then, in one war, chose a spelling that put it on the
      Boer side of a family argument.</p>
    <p>The men are Booysen — <b>Gerthardus Lodewikus</b>, then four <b>Petrus Jacobuses</b>, then
      <b>Willem Hermanus</b>, at Graaff-Reinet and Cradock and Barkly East. The women are not.
      <b>Mountjoy</b> (1838). <b>Barry</b> (1879). <b>Kinnear</b> (1940s). Behind the Barrys,
      <b>Kolbe</b> — a German tailor's family out of Soho who sailed as <b>1820 Settlers</b> and named a
      Karoo farm <em>Wurtemburg</em>. Behind the Kolbes, <b>Downing</b>.</p>
    <p>That is why David Defranceski's great-grandfather, born on a farm in the Eastern Cape in 1901, was
      christened <b>George Downing Mountjoy Booyzen</b>. Every one of those forenames is a woman's
      surname. <a href={u("/women/")}>The women &rarr;</a></p>
    <p>And it is why the tree folds back on itself: in 1924 <b>George married his own first cousin once
      removed</b>, so his daughter Catherine descends from one Barry–Kolbe couple twice over.
      <a href={u("/cousins/")}>The cousin marriage &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:26px">Corrections are the most valuable thing anyone can send. A
    family member with a date, a photograph of a document, or a flat contradiction of something written
    here will change this archive, and the change will be recorded rather than quietly made.</div>
</section>

{/* The six sibling archives are listed, linked and coloured by the footer the
    kit renders below. This section used to name three of them in bold with no
    links, immediately above it. What is worth keeping is the one fact the
    footer cannot carry: which marriage joins this archive to another. */}
<section class="blk">
  <p class="dek">Catherine Mary Sophia Booyzen's marriage to Nuno Fernando Lerena in 1949 is the
    hinge between this archive and <a href="https://daviddef.github.io/TheLerena/">The Lerena</a>.</p>
</section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What this archive has established</h2>
    <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>The letter was changed on purpose</h3>
        <p><strong>Booyzen</strong>, not Booysen. The family says the spelling was changed during
        the Anglo-Boer War, to avoid being taken for men who fought on the British side — and the
        surname statistics agree with the family. <a href={u("/name/")}>The name →</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>The Kolbes end in London, not the Cape</h3>
        <p>Johan Gottlieb Kolbe died at <strong>34 Conduit Street on 18 November 1845</strong> and
        was buried at the German Savoy Church eight days later, aged 81. The line runs out of the
        Cape and into a German congregation in Mayfair. <a href={u("/kolbe/")}>The Kolbes →</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>They married their cousins, and it is documented</h3>
        <p>Not lore: the marriages are on the register, and the archive sets out which couples were
        related and how closely. <a href={u("/cousins/")}>The cousin marriages →</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Every place is plotted from a coordinate, not a guess</h3>
        <p>Forty-six of the fifty-one places carry a latitude and longitude, a narrative and the
        people who stood in them; the five that do not are named rather than quietly dropped.
        <a href={u("/atlas/")}>The atlas →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

<Numbers rows={stats.map(([n, l, h]) => [n, l, u(h)])} />

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>What is still missing</h2>
    <p class="dek"><strong>Thirteen questions</strong>, several answered and one closed — and the
    oldest still open. Who Gerthardus Lodewikus Booysen's parents were; whether the County Cork the
    family speaks of is real at all. Three enquiries are out and unanswered: the Western Cape
    Archives, the National Archives in Pretoria, and the SANDF Documentation Centre, which has
    delivered half of what was asked. The list, and what would close each one, is at
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions</a>.</p>
    <p class="small" style="margin-top:1rem">Nothing here is taken on trust because it appears in a
    family tree. Where a record has been seen, it is cited; where it has not, the claim is marked.
    See <a href={u("/method/")}>Method</a>.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
